
---
import MainLayout from "../../layouts/MainLayout.astro";
---

<MainLayout
  title="Loss Reserve Chain Ladder Model | Eddie Zhu"
  description="An actuarial claims reserving project using Excel, R and Python."
>

  <!-- PROJECT INTRODUCTION -->

  <section class="project-hero">
    <div class="container">

      <a href="/projects" class="back-link">
        ← Back to Projects
      </a>

      <p class="eyebrow">ACTUARIAL RESERVING</p>

      <h1>Loss Reserve Chain Ladder Model</h1>

      <p class="subtitle">
        Developing an Excel-based actuarial reserving model
        and independently validating calculations using R
        and Python.
      </p>

      <div class="technologies">
        <span>Excel</span>
        <span>VBA</span>
        <span>R</span>
        <span>Python</span>
        <span>Chain Ladder</span>
      </div>

      <a
        href="https://github.com/Eddie-Zhu-IRL/Loss-Reserve-Chain-Ladder-Model"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener noreferrer"
        class="button"
      >
        View GitHub Repository ↗
      </a>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- PROJECT OBJECTIVE -->

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">

      <h2>1. Project Objective</h2>

      <p>
        Claims reserving is an important actuarial process
        in general insurance. Insurers need to estimate
        outstanding claims liabilities for claims that have
        occurred but have not yet been fully settled.
      </p>

      <p>
        The objective of this project is to develop an
        Excel-based reserving tool using the Chain Ladder
        method to estimate ultimate claims and outstanding
        reserves from historical claims development data.
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>Construct cumulative loss development triangles from historical claims data. </li>
        <li>Calculate Age-to-Age Loss Development Factors. </li>
        <li>Estimate Age-to-Ultimate Factors. </li>
        <li>Project Ultimate Losses using the Chain Ladder method. </li>
        <li>Estimate Outstanding Reserves and IBNR. </li>
        <li>Validate reserving calculations by independently implementing the methodology in Excel, VBA, Python and R. </li>

      </ul>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- METHODOLOGY -->

  <section class="section alternate">
    <div class="container">

      <h2>2. Actuarial Methodology</h2>

      <p>
        The Chain Ladder method uses historical claims
        development patterns to estimate future claims
        development.

        This project uses the Casualty Actuarial Society (CAS) PP Auto Data Set (.csv), updated December 2025.  
        <a href="https://www.casact.org/publications-research/research/research-resources/loss-reserving-data-pulled-naic-schedule-p">
          <strong>(CAS: PP Auto)</strong> 
        </a>
      </p>

      <div class="method-grid">

        <article>
          <h3>Claims Development Triangle</h3>
          <p>
            Organise historical cumulative claims by
            accident year and development period.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article>
          <h3>Age-to-Age Factors</h3>
          <p>
            Calculate development factors from successive
            development periods.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article>
          <h3>Cumulative Development Factors</h3>
          <p>
            Calculate cumulative development factors
            (CDFs) to project losses to ultimate.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article>
          <h3>Ultimate Losses and Reserves</h3>
          <p>
            Estimate ultimate losses and derive outstanding
            reserves by comparing ultimate and observed
            cumulative losses.
          </p>
        </article>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- IMPLEMENTATION -->

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">

      <h2>3. Technical Implementation</h2>

      <div class="implementation-grid">

        <article>
          <h3>Loss Triangle: Excel Built-in Functions</h3>
          <p>
            This implementation demonstrates a fully dynamic reserving model without requiring manual recalculation.
            Features include:
            <ul>
            <li>Dynamic loss development triangle.</li>
            <li>Dynamic accident year expansion.</li>
            <li>Named Ranges.</li>
            <li>INDEX/MATCH.</li>
            <li>SUMIFS.</li>
            <li>Conditional Formatting.</li>
            <li>Automatic recalculation of reserving metrics.</li>
            </ul>
          </p>
        </article>

        <article>
          <h3>Loss Triangle: VBA</h3>
          <p>
            The VBA version automates Loss Triangle and LDF workflow..
            Features include:
            <ul>
            <li>Loss Data: user can use dynamic data filtering from dropdown list, and extract filtered data for further analysis.</li>
            <li>Loss Triangle: create triangle.</li>
            <li>LDF: Arithmetic AVG & Vol-Weighted AVG: calculate LDF using simple arithmetic average and volume-weighted average.</li>
            <li>Summary: consolidate earned premiums, incurred losses, cumulative paid loss, calculted Age-to-Ult LDF, Ult Losses, IBNR.</li>
            </ul>
          </p>
        </article>

        <article>
          <h3>R</h3>
          <p>
            Use the ChainLadder package to independently
            calculate reserving outputs and compare
            development factors and estimates.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article>
          <h3>Python</h3>
          <p>
            Implement an independent computational
            comparison of the Excel model outputs.
          </p>
        </article>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- VALIDATION -->

  <section class="section alternate">
    <div class="container">

      <h2>4. Results Validation and Testing</h2>

      <ul>
        <h3>4.1 Triangle construction and LDF </h3>

        <li>Excel Built-in Functions</li>
        <img
          src="/images/reserving/triangle_excel.png"
          alt="triangle_excel"
          class="triangle_excel"
        />

        <li>VBA</li>
        <img
          src="/images/reserving/triangle_vba.png"
          alt="triangle_vba"
          class="triangle_vba"
        />

      </ul>

      <ul>
        <h3>4.2 Ultimate Loss Estimation and IBNR </h3>

        <li>Excel Built-in Functions</li>
        <img
          src="/images/reserving/summary_excel.png"
          alt="summary_excel"
          class="summary_excel"
        />

        <li>VBA</li>
        <img
          src="/images/reserving/summary_vba.png"
          alt="summary_vba"
          class="summary_vba"
        />

        <li>Python</li>
        <img
          src="/images/reserving/summary_python.png"
          alt="summary_python"
          class="summary_python"
        />

        <li>R</li>
        <img
          src="/images/reserving/summary_r.png"
          alt="summary_r"
          class="summary_r"
        />

      </ul>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- LEARNING -->

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">

      <h2>5. Skills Developed</h2>

      <div class="skills-list">
        <span>Reserving</span>
        <span>Chain Ladder</span>
        <span>Loss Development</span>
        <span>Excel Modelling</span>
        <span>VBA</span>
        <span>R</span>
        <span>Python</span>
        <span>Model Validation</span>
        <span>Actuarial Analysis</span>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- GITHUB -->

  <section class="project-contact">
    <div class="container">

      <h2>Explore the Project</h2>

      <p>
        View the Excel model, outpus from R and Python for reconciliation, supporting calculations
        and technical documentation on GitHub.
      </p>

      <a
        href="https://github.com/Eddie-Zhu-IRL/Loss-Reserve-Chain-Ladder-Model"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener noreferrer"
        class="button"
      >
        Visit GitHub Repository ↗
      </a>

    </div>
  </section>

</MainLayout>


<style>
  .project-hero {
    background: var(--color-navy);
    color: white;
    padding: 75px 0;
  }

  .back-link {
    color: #BFDBFE;
    display: inline-block;
    margin-bottom: 35px;
  }

  .eyebrow {
    color: #93C5FD;
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 1px;
  }

  .project-hero h1 {
    font-size: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 3.5rem);
    max-width: 850px;
  }

  .subtitle {
    max-width: 750px;
    color: #DCE6F3;
    font-size: 1.1rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
  }

  .technologies,
  .skills-list {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 10px;
    margin: 25px 0;
  }

  .technologies span,
  .skills-list span {
    background: #EFF6FF;
    color: #1D4ED8;
    padding: 7px 12px;
    border-radius: 5px;
    font-size: 0.85rem;
  }

  .method-grid,
  .implementation-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
    gap: 20px;
    margin-top: 30px;
  }

  .method-grid article,
  .implementation-grid article {
    background: white;
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    border-radius: 8px;
    padding: 22px;
  }

  h2 {
    color: var(--color-navy);
    font-size: 1.8rem;
  }

  h3 {
    color: var(--color-navy);
  }

  .alternate {
    background: #EDF2F8;
  }

  .section p,
  .section li {
    color: var(--color-muted);
    line-height: 1.8;
  }

  .project-contact {
    background: var(--color-navy);
    color: white;
    text-align: center;
    padding: 65px 0;
  }

  .project-contact h2 {
    color: white;
  }

  .project-contact p {
    color: #DCE6F3;
  }

  @media (max-width: 650px) {
    .method-grid,
    .implementation-grid {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }
</style>
